\section{Error Analysis \emph{[Exploratory]}}
\label{sec:analysis}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_type_mechanism.pdf}
\caption{Type--mechanism association (percentage of violation traces per type assigned to each failure mechanism). Each type maps to a dominant mechanism ($\geq$3/5 model consistency), supporting the taxonomy's diagnostic value (NMI $= 0.23$, Cram\'{e}r's $V = 0.45$, LLM-coded; human collapsed $\kappa = 0.34$--$0.52$).}
\label{fig:type_mechanism}
\end{figure}

The following failure mode taxonomy emerges empirically from LLM-coded traces, distinct from the input ambiguity taxonomy (\S\ref{sec:taxonomy}).

We analyze 614 violation traces across all five models, coded into five failure modes (Gwet's AC$_1$ $= 0.62$, $\kappa = 0.52$ \citep{gwet_2014,cohen_1960}); the coder prompt excludes ambiguity type labels, with type assigned post hoc. Using LLMs to code LLM failures risks circularity; however, type--mechanism mutual information (NMI $= 0.23$; Cram\'{e}r's $V = 0.45$, bootstrap 95\% CI $[0.41, 0.50]$; Appendix~\ref{app:type_mechanism}) provides structural evidence against this.\footnote{$V$ is computed on a $6\times 5$ table vs.\ $6\times 2$ for severity; higher dimensionality makes $V = 0.45$ conservative.} Human mechanism coding yields $\kappa = 0.15$, rising to $0.34$--$0.52$ after collapsing the spurious-compliance/structural-misparse boundary (Appendix~\ref{app:human_validation}); we treat $V = 0.45$ as an upper bound. The higher inter-coder agreement among LLMs ($\kappa = 0.52$) than humans ($\kappa = 0.15$) may partly reflect shared classification conventions---a meta-level parallel to the convergent nocuity documented below---so the following patterns are best interpreted as categorization tendencies rather than ground-truth assignments. Two of six mappings reflect partial definitional affinity (authorization scope$\to$boundary violation, incompleteness$\to$gap-filling); the remaining four (e.g., scopal$\to$structural misparse, 82.7\% consistency) are empirically derived.

\subsection{Type-Specific Failure Mechanisms}

Three failure patterns emerge ($\geq$3/5 cross-model consistency; Appendix~\ref{app:failure_details}): \emph{structural misparse}---incorrect parse commitment \citep{frazier_rayner_1982}---dominates scopal (5/5), conditional precedence (4/5), and coreferential (3/5); \emph{information gap-filling}---plausible but incorrect defaults---dominates incompleteness (3/5) and lexical (3/5); and \emph{boundary violation}---unauthorized escalation (69.2\%, $p < 10^{-4}$)---dominates authorization scope exclusively (5/5).

\enlargethispage{28\baselineskip}

\subsection{Disambiguation Neglect}
\label{sec:dn}

Agents rarely recognize ambiguity: overall awareness is 4.6\%, with GPT-4.1 and Claude at 10--11\% while Qwen3 and DeepSeek show zero ($p = 2.2 \times 10^{-9}$; Appendix~\ref{app:awareness_breakdown}). We term this \emph{disambiguation neglect}---extending ambiguity collapse \citep{liu_ambiguity_2023,hu_ambiguity_2025} from the representational level to the behavioral level: agents act on collapsed readings without recognizing ambiguity, producing irreversible safety violations rather than mere accuracy degradation. Convergent nocuity (\S\ref{sec:nocuity}) further extends to the population level. A prompt-based mitigation (Appendix~\ref{app:mitigation}) confirms type-dependent effectiveness: authorization scope ($-$18.0\,pp), lexical ($-$8.9\,pp), but not incompleteness ($-$3.4\,pp, n.s.).

\subsection{Convergent Nocuity}
\label{sec:nocuity}

We adopt `nocuous' from \citet{berry_kamsties_2004}, but inverted: our agents exhibit \emph{convergent} rather than \emph{divergent} errors. Among ambiguous clauses, 41.3\% trigger majority-convergent violations ($\geq$3/5 models; 13.0\% unanimous)---we term this \emph{convergent nocuity}, invisible in agreement-based evaluation. These convergent violations exhibit a \emph{permissive attractor}: 77.0\% reflect permissive interpretation ($p \approx 0$; Appendix~\ref{app:attractor}). Cross-family violation co-occurrence (mean Jaccard $= 0.46$, $n = 9$ pairs) approaches within-family levels ($0.54$, $n = 1$), with three cross-family pairs within $0.05$ of the within-family rate, indicating convergent violations reflect shared architectural biases rather than training-data overlap (Appendix~\ref{app:cross_family}).

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Disambiguation neglect and prompt-level safety.}
The 4.6\% ambiguity awareness rate implies prompt-level safety instructions are insufficient: agents cannot flag ambiguity they do not recognize. Type-specific mitigation confirms this: authorization scope ($-$18.0\,pp) responds to explicit instructions, but incompleteness ($-$3.4\,pp, n.s.) does not, because missing information cannot be recovered from the prompt alone; a second prompt formulation worsens incompleteness by $+$14\,pp ($p = 0.118$), confirming that prompt-level intervention is insufficient for informational gaps (Appendix~\ref{app:mitigation}).

\paragraph{Convergent nocuity and agreement-based evaluation.}
The 41.3\% convergent violation rate challenges multi-agent agreement as a safety proxy: cross-family violation co-occurrence (Jaccard $= 0.46$) approaches within-family levels ($0.54$). Unlike sycophancy, which requires exposure to a preferred answer, convergent nocuity arises from shared structural biases---the 77\% permissive attractor operates without user pressure or inter-model communication---so multi-agent consensus may actively mask systematic failures as corroborated judgments.

\paragraph{Practical implications.}
Developers should conduct clause-level ambiguity audits before deployment rather than type-level screening (Appendix~\ref{app:detection}). The type--mechanism dissociation maps types to targeted fixes: numerical thresholds for authorization scope ($-$36.0\,pp), structural disambiguation for scopal clauses, and default-value specification for incompleteness. The $\tau^2$-bench effect ($+$34.0\,pp) provides an upper bound and the confound-minimized coreferential baseline ($+$18.7\,pp) a lower bound; SOPBench ($+$12.7\,pp unweighted mean; Appendix~\ref{app:sopbench}) approximates naturally occurring ambiguity---possibly reflecting the smaller ambiguity--information gap in real-world policies.

